--TEST--
A method whose body contains `yield` may not declare a `&$x` parameter, while an ordinary method beside it still may — ADR 0053 § 4
--FILE--
<?mwl
// A `&$x` parameter addresses a cell the *call site* stages for the duration
// of the call, and what makes that sound is that the callee's frame dies
// first. A generator inverts it: calling one runs none of the body, it
// allocates the state object and returns (ADR 0053 § 4), so the staged cell is
// gone before the first `advance()` while the parked frame would still be
// addressing it. There is no representation to keep instead — copying the
// value in would stop being a reference, which is the whole observable point.
final class Feed {
    // Reported once per by-reference parameter, not once per method.
    public static function walk(int &$seen, int &$last): Iterator<int> {
        $seen = 1;
        yield 1;
        $last = 2;
        yield 2;
    }

    // No `yield`, so this frame *is* the call — unaffected, and it is what a
    // by-reference parameter still lowers to.
    public static function bump(int &$n): void {
        $n = $n + 1;
    }
}

int $seen = 0;
foreach (Feed::walk($seen, $seen) as int $v) {
    echo $v, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0492]: a generator cannot take `$seen` by reference
   --> case.mwl:11:38
%A
error[E0492]: a generator cannot take `$last` by reference
   --> case.mwl:11:50
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
